+# Option A Implementation: Offline Dismiss Parity via Dictionary Synchronization
+
+**Date:** 2026-08-03  
+**Status:** Implementation complete (pending build verification)  
+**Approach:** Option A — Keep IAM/disqualify rule combine idiom, but fix dictionary population at init time
+
+---
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Content cards dismissed offline would reappear because the offline hydration path (`hydrateContentCardRulesEngineFromDisk`) loaded only content-card rules and **dropped the disqualify (eventHistoryOperation) rules** that rode alongside them on disk.
+
+```
+Disk persists:  { content-card rule, disqualify rule }
+Offline hydrate: Load content-card → contentCardRulesEngine
+                 Drop disqualify ✗
+                 
+Result: Dismissed card can re-qualify offline and reappear
+```
+
+---
+
+## Architectural Decision: Option A
+
+Two viable options were considered:
+
+| Option | Approach | Pros | Cons |
+|--------|----------|------|------|
+| **A** | Populate `inAppRulesBySurface` at init time, keep the "combine" idiom | Consistent with network path; minimal changes; uses existing architecture | Still combines IAM + disqualify rules (shares same engine) |
+| **C** | New dedicated offline-only event-history engine | Structurally isolated; no combine needed | New engine (inconsistent count); online/offline disqualify asymmetry |
+
+**Chosen:** Option A — aligns with AEP's existing design principle: **"one engine per evaluation semantic, rules collected into dictionaries before one full replace"** (not one engine per data source).
+
+---
+
+## Implementation Details
+
+### 1. Access Level Change: `inAppRulesBySurface` (Messaging.swift)
+
+**File:** `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift` (lines 111–120)
+
+**Change:** `private` → `internal` (computed property)
+
+**Why:** `Messaging+State.swift` (a different file) needs to populate this dictionary during IAM's init-time hydration. In Swift, `private` is file-scoped, so the visibility change is necessary to share state across extension files.
+
+**Precedent:** Other rule-collection properties already use internal visibility (`qualifiedContentCardsBySurface`, `contentCardOriginByProposition`).
+
+---
+
+### 2. Dictionary Population: `hydratePropositionsRulesEngine()` (Messaging+State.swift)
+
+**File:** `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging+State.swift` (lines 72–79)
+
+**Change Added:**
+```swift
+private func hydratePropositionsRulesEngine() {
+    let parsedPropositions = ParsedPropositions(with: inMemoryPropositions, ...)
+    if let inAppRules = parsedPropositions.surfaceRulesBySchemaType[.inapp] {
+        // NEW: Record loaded IAM rules in the dictionary
+        for (surface, rules) in inAppRules {
+            inAppRulesBySurface[surface] = rules
+        }
+        rulesEngine.launchRulesEngine.replaceRules(with: inAppRules.flatMap { $0.value })
+    }
+    updatePropositionInfo(parsedPropositions.propositionInfoToCache)
+}
+```
+
+**Why:** 
+- At `init()` time, IAM rules are loaded directly into `rulesEngine` but the dictionary stays empty
+- Later, content-card offline hydration rebuilds the shared engine by collecting from `inAppRulesBySurface`
+- Without this update, the dictionary read yields an empty set → `replaceRules(combined)` clobbers IAM rules
+- This mirrors what the network path already does in `updateRulesEngines` → `processRulesForSchemaType(.inapp, ...)`
+
+---
+
+### 3. Disqualify Rule Loading: `hydrateContentCardRulesEngineFromDisk()` (Messaging.swift)
+
+**File:** `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift` (lines 1387–1395)
+
+**Already staged; now works correctly because `inAppRulesBySurface` is populated:**
+
+```swift
+if let eventHistoryRules = parsedPropositions.surfaceRulesBySchemaType[.eventHistoryOperation],
+   !eventHistoryRules.isEmpty {
+    for (surface, rules) in eventHistoryRules {
+        eventHistoryRulesBySurface[surface] = rules
+    }
+    var combined = collectRules(from: inAppRulesBySurface)       // ← now accurate
+    combined.append(contentsOf: collectRules(from: eventHistoryRulesBySurface))
+    rulesEngine.launchRulesEngine.replaceRules(with: combined)  // ← preserves both
+}
+```
+
+**Flow:**
+1. Parse disqualify rules from persisted content-card propositions
+2. Load them into the `eventHistoryRulesBySurface` dictionary
+3. Collect IAM rules from `inAppRulesBySurface` (now guaranteed populated)
+4. Combine both and rebuild the shared engine in one atomic `replaceRules` call
+
